<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
  <!--
    The same group the app and the widget extension name, and the ONLY reason
    this extension can hand anything over: a share extension is its own
    sandboxed process that is killed the moment its sheet closes, so a file it
    writes into its own container is a file nothing will ever read.

    `plugin/with-hermie-share.js` refuses to prebuild when this string is not the
    one the app uses. The failure it is guarding against reports itself nowhere:
    the extension writes into a container of its own, the app looks in one with
    nothing in it, and sharing silently does nothing at all.
  -->
  <key>com.apple.security.application-groups</key>
  <array>
    <string>group.dev.hermie.app</string>
  </array>
  <!--
    And the keychain group the app writes its delivery credential into.

    This is what ADR-0026 needed that ADR-0023 did not: the container above
    carries what a bot is CALLED, and a bearer token has no business in a plain
    directory every binary in the group can read. So the credential is a keychain
    item, and an item is only readable by a binary that declares the group it was
    written into.

    `$(AppIdentifierPrefix)dev.hermie.app` is the app's FIRST group — it is what
    `ios.entitlements` in app.config.ts names, and `expo-secure-store` writes
    without an explicit access group, which means writes land in the first entry.
    A read searches every declared group, so naming the app's here is enough and
    nothing has to name a group on the write side.

    Same silence as the App Group if it is wrong: the lookup finds nothing, the
    extension queues every share, and nothing anywhere says why. The plugin
    asserts the pairing for that reason.
  -->
  <key>keychain-access-groups</key>
  <array>
    <string>$(AppIdentifierPrefix)dev.hermie.app</string>
  </array>
</dict>
</plist>
